<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Wenxuan of Northern Qi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">526–559</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Gao Yang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Wenxuan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a>, born Gao Yang, was a son of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a>, who had dominated the Eastern Wei. In 550 he forced Emperor Xiaojing to abdicate and founded the Northern Qi dynasty with its capital at Ye. His early reign was energetic, but his later years were notorious for drunkenness and cruelty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi.jpg" alt="Emperor Wenxuan of Northern Qi" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 张居正 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4_%E9%AB%98%E6%B8%85_(page_131_%E9%AB%98%E6%B4%8B).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gao Yang was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> and his wife Lou Zhaojun, of a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> family; his courtesy name was Zijin and his Xianbei nickname Hounigan. He was created Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> in 535. As a child he seemed awkward and slow, apparently feigning it so as not to arouse his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-cheng">Gao Cheng</a>'s suspicion. Asked by his father to untangle a ball of yarn, he cut it open with his sword, and when the general Peng Le staged a mock attack on the brothers, he alone stayed calm and captured Peng.</p><p>In 549 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-cheng">Gao Cheng</a>, regent of the Eastern Wei since their father's death in 547, was assassinated in Yecheng by his servant Lan Jing. Gao Yang, who was in the city, killed the conspirators, kept the death secret while he took control, and secured the family's stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinyang">Jinyang</a>. In 550 he was made Prince of Qi, and later that year Emperor Xiaojing yielded the throne to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign he revised the laws, formed border corps from elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and Han soldiers and divided households into nine classes by wealth to share out taxes and labour. He repulsed an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumo-xi">Kumo Xi</a> in 552 and the Khitan in 553, and ordered a great wall built in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>. Around the new year of 552 he had the former emperor poisoned and his three sons killed.</p><p>Traditional historians, among them Li Yanshou and Sima Guang, saw his campaign of 554 against the Shanhu as the turning point: he ordered every Shanhu male aged eleven or over killed. Thereafter, driven by alcoholism and possibly an untreated psychiatric disorder, he killed often and cruelly, among them his concubine Consort Xue, his brothers Gao Jun and Gao Huàn, whom he had imprisoned, and, in 559, members of the Northern Wei imperial Yuan clan regardless of age or sex. In 555 he merged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and executed four Taoist monks who resisted. Yet the prime minister Yang Yin ran an efficient government, and officials feared to be corrupt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the autumn of 559 Wenxuan fell gravely ill, an illness historians believe was driven by alcoholism. He told his brother Gao Yan to take the throne but not to kill his son, yet left the succession unchanged, and his son Gao Yin succeeded him as Emperor Fei. At his death only Yang Yin was able to shed a tear. Not long after, Gao Yan killed Yang Yin, deposed Gao Yin and became emperor. Despite his rages, his reign, with the help of his advisers, was considered well administered and effective.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Wenxuan of Northern Qi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Another son, <b>Gao Yang</b>, forced Emperor Xiaojing to abdicate in 550 and founded the Northern Qi.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-wei">Eastern Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>In 550 his son <b>Gao Yang</b> deposed the last Eastern Wei ruler and proclaimed himself emperor of Qi at Ye, inheriting the wealthy and populous plains of Hebei, Henan and Shanxi, with a second power centre at Jinyang.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a></li></ul></section>
